// ALG1 Unit 05 Lesson 01 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Polynomial Operations and Radical Readiness
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.FND.3; MLA.A1.FND.6; MLA.A1.FND.7

::ALG1_U05_L01_GP_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_GP_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((3x^2+4x-1)+(2x^2-x+5)\).
{
=5x^2+3x+4#Correct. Combine like terms: \(3x^2+2x^2=5x^2\), \(4x-x=3x\), and \(-1+5=4\).
~5x^4+3x+4#Adding like terms does not add exponents. The \(x^2\) terms stay degree 2.
~x^2+5x+4#This subtracts the \(x^2\) coefficients instead of adding them.
~5x^2+5x-6#This combines signs incorrectly for the x-terms and constants.
}

::ALG1_U05_L01_GP_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_GP_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.3]
What is \(\sqrt{81}\)?
{
~8#This is close, but \(8^2=64\), not 81.
=9#Correct. \(9^2=81\), so \(\sqrt{81}=9\).
~-9#The principal square root symbol asks for the nonnegative square root.
~40.5#This divides 81 by 2 instead of finding the number whose square is 81.
}

::ALG1_U05_L01_GP_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_GP_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \(2x(3x+4)\).
{
~6x+8#This multiplies coefficients but does not include \(x\cdot x=x^2\) in the first term.
~5x+8#This adds \(2x\) and \(3x\) instead of multiplying.
=6x^2+8x#Correct. Distribute \(2x\): \(2x\cdot3x=6x^2\) and \(2x\cdot4=8x\).
~6x^2+4#The second term must also be multiplied by \(2x\).
}

::ALG1_U05_L01_GP_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_GP_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.7]
Simplify \((12x^3+6x^2)/(3x)\).
{
~4x^3+2x^2#This divides coefficients but does not subtract exponents.
~9x^2+3x#This subtracts coefficients instead of dividing them.
~4x^2+2#The second term \(6x^2/(3x)\) leaves \(2x\), not 2.
=4x^2+2x#Correct. Divide each term: \(12x^3/(3x)=4x^2\) and \(6x^2/(3x)=2x\).
}

::ALG1_U05_L01_GP_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_L01_GP_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6]
Simplify \((5x^2+2)-(x^2-3)\).
{
~4x^2-1#This does not distribute the subtraction to \(-3\).
=4x^2+5#Correct. \(5x^2+2-x^2+3=4x^2+5\).
~6x^2-1#This adds the \(x^2\) terms and subtracts the constants.
~4x^2-5#This changes the sign of 2 as well as the second expression.
}
